  "final_span": "after a country park or protecting a wetland, should a conservation organisation have more say over what that farmer is doing?\u201d\nMr. Ward highlighted the In My Shoes project, which aims to return public funding for environmental schemes to local communities through the creation of \u201cadministering funds.\u201d\nMr Ward said the public funds\u2019 money should support community and regional insights organisations, to bring \u201cconsumers\u2019 claims into being, helping them make sustainable and responsible choices\u201d.\nFarmers from the local area could then submit bids for public funding, showcasing plans for ongoing environmental efforts.\nMr. Duffy stated that the UK was moving toward having what could be called three types of land\u2014private, state, and communal. He worried that the funds meant for environmental enhancements could be allocated to the farming community he claimed would manage the third type of common land.\nHe agreed in a sense with Mr Ward on the idea",
